\begin_layout Chapter
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Press a button, open the bellows a little, and at first nothing happens. Air goes through, but there is no note. Push a little harder and, suddenly, the reed speaks. Behind this small event hides a strange machine: a steady stream of air is turned into a regular vibration, hundreds of times per second, and the vibration starts by itself.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This part follows one steel tongue, from its shape at rest to the sound it gives. Six questions, written by Ewen before the work began, form its plan. They are kept as they were asked. Each section below gives a first, short answer, and says where this part develops it; the chapters that follow tell how far each answer can be trusted. For a general introduction to the acoustics of free-reed instruments, see Cottingham 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "cottingham2011"
literal "true"

\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Q1 : How the sound is created with free reeds ?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A free reed is a thin steel tongue riveted over a slot cut in a metal plate. The tongue can swing through the slot, up and down, without touching its edges. The gap around it is tiny: about 30 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset
m in the models of this thesis, a value that is still to be measured. This is what makes the reed free. A clarinet reed is different: it beats against the mouthpiece and closes it. A free reed never closes its slot completely. It only makes the opening wider and narrower.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So the reed is a valve. Each time the tongue leaves the slot, a puff of air goes through. Each time it comes back, the flow is cut down. The note we hear is mostly this chopped air, as in a siren. All the models of this part make this hypothesis: the sound is radiated by the pulsed flow, like a small source that breathes in and out, and the far-field sound pressure follows the rate of change of the flow, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{d}q/\mathrm{d}t$
\end_inset
, not the pressure inside the chamber. The soundboard, the box and the bellows also radiate; they are left aside here (Part Other parts involved).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A free reed speaks in one direction only. When the air comes the other way, a small leather valve closes the slot. This is why each button of an accordion has one reed for pushing and another one for pulling. The models found that this one-way opening is not a detail: a symmetric opening, which lets the air through on both sides of the plate, modulates the flow twice per period, and a reed tuned at 220 Hz then sang at 470 Hz, one octave too high.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where does the energy come from? The flow is steady. The tongue is a spring, and a spring left alone comes to rest. Something in the way the air and the tongue talk to each other must give the tongue a small push at the right moment of each cycle, like a child pushed on a swing. Finding this push is the central question of this part. The chapter Static and modal analysis studies the swing, the tongue alone. The chapter Self-sustained conditions studies the push. The answer is not complete yet: the simplest models reproduce the normal accordion reed, but they fail on another reed that plays anyway (Section A physical model for reed design).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Q2 : What is the coupling method of reed and cavity ?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Under each reed there is a small room of air: the chamber of the reed block. The tongue and this room exchange forces. When the tongue opens the slot, air leaves the room (or enters it, when the bellows pulls), the pressure in the room changes, and this pressure pushes back on the tongue. The air of the chamber acts as a spring. The air in the holes around it, in the soundboard and under the pallet, acts as small masses. The question is: in this exchange, who leads?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two answers were tried. In the first one, the chamber is the partner: the tongue would sing because the chamber gives the push back at the right time. The computations say no for a chamber vented by its hole in the soundboard. With the guessed dimensions of the default model, the resonance of the chamber and its hole is near 1.9 kHz, far above most notes. Below that resonance, the chamber does not store the push and give it back late; it lets the air escape, and it damps the tongue instead of feeding it. This agrees with Ricot, Caussé and Misdariis 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "ricot2005"
literal "true"

\end_inset
: an accordion reed starts even without acoustic coupling, through the aerodynamics of the flow in its slot.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the second answer, the coupling is local. It sits in the slot itself and in the masses of air just around it. If the opening closes as the tongue moves into the slot, these masses turn a slowing of the flow into an overpressure in phase with the velocity of the tongue, and the tongue is fed. This mechanism reproduces the normal accordion reed. It does not explain a reed mounted between two plates, which also plays. Section A physical model for reed design tells this story, and Section Multi reed coupling follows two reeds that share their air. Part Reed blocks coupling studies the chamber itself in more detail.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Q3 : Are the reeds working in least energy state ?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A stone rolls down to the bottom of a valley and stays there. This is a state of least energy. A sounding reed is not like that. It is an open system: at every cycle it takes energy from the air, and it loses energy in its steel, in its rivet, in the turbulent air and in the sound. Its steady note is a balance, not a rest. Physicists call it a limit cycle: the closed path that the motion keeps following, because on this path the energy gained during one period equals the energy lost. When the bellows stops, the gain disappears and the tongue falls back to rest, its real state of least energy, within a few seconds at most.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this strict sense, the answer to Q3 is no. But the question hides a better one: among all the ways a reed could vibrate, does it choose the one that wastes the least? This part answers what it can with the energy balance of the tongue, in the sections Stored energy and Dissipation. Part Reed blocks coupling comes back to it with the chamber (chapter Entropy and least energy state).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): what did you mean by least energy state when you wrote this question: the lowest stored energy, the lowest power lost for a given loudness, or the lowest production of entropy? The answer decides how Part Reed blocks coupling treats it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Q4 : The drawback and advantages of using a bellow ?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The accordion is fed by a bellows moved by the arm. This is not a detail. A bellows pushes a volume of air per second, more than it holds a pressure: it behaves more like a source of flow than a source of pressure. The proof is in the hands of every player. Keep the bellows speed constant and open a chord: the pressure drops, because the same flow is now shared by more reeds. A pressure source, like the large reservoir of an organ, would keep the pressure and send twice the flow through two reeds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The advantages are musical. The player controls the air directly, with nuance and accent, and the bellows itself becomes a voice: the bellow shake, a fast push-pull movement of the bellows, is a playing effect. The drawbacks are physical. The air is limited by the size of the bellows. The direction must change, and each change is a short break and a change of reeds. The folds of the bellows give a little under pressure, and they leak.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the models, the truth lies between the two ideal sources. A real source gives less flow when the pressure rises. The simplest law is a straight line, 
\begin_inset Formula $q=q_{0}-p/R$
\end_inset
, where 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{0}$
\end_inset
 is the flow at zero pressure and 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset
 is the internal impedance of the source, in Pa
\begin_inset Formula $\cdot$
\end_inset
s/m
\begin_inset Formula $^{3}$
\end_inset
. A vertical line on the 
\begin_inset Formula $(q,p)$
\end_inset
 plane is an ideal flow source; a horizontal line is an ideal pressure source. The slope is the number the model needs. It can be measured on the bench with the flow meter and the pressure sensor, by sweeping the opening at a fixed drive and plotting 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset
 against 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset
 (experiment E1 of the list of experiments). An even simpler test settles the question of the type of source in ten minutes: at a fixed drive, open one reed, then two, then three, and read 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset
. A flow source keeps 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset
 and lets 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset
 drop; a pressure source keeps 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset
 and lets 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset
 grow. The sections Dissipation and Hysteresis vs self sustained conditions show how the source enters the losses and the thresholds of the reed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the 
\begin_inset Formula $(q,p)$
\end_inset
 curve of the bench turbine and, if possible, of the bellows moved by hand, to replace the provisional value of 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset
 in the models.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Q5 : What about the self sustained conditions ?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Self-sustained means that the reed keeps vibrating by itself, as long as the air flows, with no hand to pluck it again. Three things must meet. First, a source of energy: the steady flow. Second, a valve: the tongue, which modulates the flow. Third, the right timing: the force of the air on the tongue must have a part in phase with its velocity, so that the air pushes the tongue in the direction it is already moving, like the push on a swing given at the right moment. When this push is larger than the losses, the rest position becomes unstable, and the smallest disturbance grows: the reed starts. This change of behaviour is called a Hopf bifurcation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two pressures frame the life of a note, and every accordionist knows both. Below the starting pressure 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\mathrm{on}}$
\end_inset
 nothing sounds. Above the choking pressure, the tongue is blown too far out of its slot, it no longer modulates the flow, and the note dies. A third pressure, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\mathrm{off}}$
\end_inset
, appears when the pressure is lowered on a sounding reed: if the reed keeps sounding below 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\mathrm{on}}$
\end_inset
 and stops only at 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\mathrm{off}}<p_{\mathrm{on}}$
\end_inset
, there is a zone where the reed can be either silent or sounding, depending on its past. This memory is called hysteresis. The chapter Self-sustained conditions develops these three pressures.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Q6 : It is possible to create a Poly-Dynamic Instrument ?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A free reed lives in a narrow window. Below its starting pressure it is silent. Above its choking pressure it dies. And in between, its pitch drifts with the pressure: in every version of the models of this part, the note goes down when the player pushes harder (chapter Temporal and frequency of free reed). A poly-dynamic instrument would widen this window: keep its tuning and its timbre over a wider range of loudness, or give the player new ways to shape the sound. Ewen's design goals of 2018, written for the soundboard of the Accordina and the Octodyna, already pointed this way: the same air consumption in the bass as in the treble, a starting threshold low enough to allow a pianissimo over the whole range, a homogeneous timbre, and an attack shorter than 50 ms (research/legacy/plans-experience-2018-2022).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What can be measured now: the drift of pitch with pressure, with the tuner of the repository and a water manometer, and the way timbre changes with loudness, as a table of notes by nuances (experiments E1 of the two-reed audit and E11 of the list of experiments). The sections Frequency shift, Intensity and Harmonic Saturation come back to this question with what the models and the bench can already say.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): what does poly-dynamic mean for you: a wider range of loudness at constant pitch, several reeds per note that take over one another as the pressure grows, or a control of timbre that does not depend on loudness? The answer sets the design goals of Parts II to V.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Static and modal analysis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Static analysis of free reeds
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Before a reed sings, it rests. Its shape at rest decides where the tongue starts from, and so how the air meets it at the very first moment. A steady pressure also bends it before any vibration begins. This section looks at the tongue standing still.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Static shape of free reeds reeds
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At rest, the tip of the tongue is not in the plane of the plate: it is lifted. In the models, it is lifted on the side the air comes from, so that the first breath pushes the tongue into its slot. Whether real reeds are set this way is one of the open questions of this part (Section A physical model for reed design). The lift is defined as the height of the underside of the tip above the top of the plate, at mid-width, with the reed at rest and the plate lying flat. A simple protocol measures it the same way every time: a flat glass plate or a marble, a dial gauge reading to 0.01 mm (or a depth gauge), a set of feeler gauges from 0.05 to 1 mm, and a lamp behind the plate (research/docs/protocole_recalage_experimental.md).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The whole profile can be measured with cheap means. The tool profile.py moves a displacement sensor along the reed with the stepper table of the bench: a dial gauge read by hand, a low-cost analog distance sensor, an inductive sensor, or even a photograph whose edge is found by OpenCV. From the profile it computes the curvature, the second derivative of the shape, and the largest deflection at rest.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now blow on a tongue while holding it so that it cannot vibrate. A steady pressure difference 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta p$
\end_inset
, spread over its face, bends it like a diving board under a uniform load. For a uniform tongue of length 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset
 and thickness 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
, beam theory gives the deflection of the tip:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\delta=\frac{3\,\Delta p\,L^{4}}{2\,E\,t^{3}}
\]

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset
 is the Young modulus of the steel. The width has disappeared: a wider tongue receives more force, but it is stiffer in the same ratio. For a steel tongue of the size used in the models for an A4 (24 mm long, 0.3 mm thick), at 1000 Pa, this gives about 0.09 mm. This is small compared with the lift assumed in the models, 1.5 thicknesses or 0.45 mm (a guessed value), but not negligible: the steady pressure alone moves the tip about a fifth of the way towards its slot before any vibration. The fourth power of the length makes long tongues much more sensitive: at the same thickness, doubling the length multiplies the static deflection by sixteen. Bass reeds rest much closer to their slot under pressure than treble reeds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the rest lift and the static profile of the reference reeds of the reed bank, measured with the protocol above and profile.py, and the side of the lift relative to the air (pushing and pulling reeds).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Modal analysis of free reeds
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Pluck a reed with a fingernail, without air. It rings for a moment and dies away. What you hear is the tongue moving in its own natural ways, its modes. In the first mode, the whole tongue swings like a diving board; this mode sets the pitch of the note. In the next ones, the tongue bends with one or two still points along its length, or twists. They are hidden in the attack and in the timbre. This section explains how the modes depend on the shape of the tongue, and why the sound of a reed is harmonic even though its modes are not.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Mechanical theory of free reeds
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A tongue is thin: a few tenths of a millimetre for some tens of millimetres of length (0.3 mm for 24 mm in the A4 example). For such a beam, each cross-section stays flat as the beam bends, and only two properties matter: how hard it resists bending, its bending stiffness 
\begin_inset Formula $EI$
\end_inset
, and how heavy it is per metre, 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho A$
\end_inset
. Here 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset
 is the Young modulus, 
\begin_inset Formula $I=bt^{3}/12$
\end_inset
 the second moment of area of a section of width 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset
 and thickness 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset
 the density and 
\begin_inset Formula $A=bt$
\end_inset
 the area of the section. The Euler-Bernoulli equation says that bending stiffness and inertia balance at every point of the tongue:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
EI\,\frac{\partial^{4}w}{\partial x^{4}}+\rho A\,\frac{\partial^{2}w}{\partial t^{2}}=0
\]

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $w(x,t)$
\end_inset
 is the displacement of the tongue at the distance 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset
 from the rivet. At the rivet the tongue is clamped: no displacement, no slope. At the tip it is free: no bending moment, no shear force. Only some frequencies satisfy all four conditions. For a uniform tongue they are
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f_{n}=\frac{(\beta_{n}L)^{2}}{2\pi L^{2}}\sqrt{\frac{EI}{\rho A}}=\frac{(\beta_{n}L)^{2}\,t}{2\pi L^{2}}\sqrt{\frac{E}{12\,\rho}},\qquad\beta_{1}L=1.875,\;\beta_{2}L=4.694,\;\beta_{3}L=7.855
\]

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Read it in words. The stiffness grows like the cube of the thickness and the mass like the thickness, so the frequency grows like the thickness. The length enters squared: a tongue twice as long sounds two octaves lower. The width cancels out. The material enters only through 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{E/\rho}$
\end_inset
, a property of the steel itself.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Real tongues are not uniform. The repository describes a tongue as a series of sections from the rivet to the tip, each with its length, its width and its thickness at both ends, and its material (banc_recherche/languette.py, after Ewen's tool of 2020). The same description feeds three independent computations: the exact Euler-Bernoulli solution by transfer matrices 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "pestel1963"
literal "true"

\end_inset
, the Rayleigh-Ritz method, and the finite elements of Elmer (Section Finite element model with fluid structure interaction). The modal code was first checked on the one case where the answer is known exactly: on a uniform beam it finds the three first frequencies of the cantilever formula (134.11, 840.46 and 2353.30 Hz in the test case) with no visible error.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Constant height reeds
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The simplest tongue has the same thickness everywhere. Its first mode follows directly from the formula above. A steel tongue 24 mm long and 0.3 mm thick, with 
\begin_inset Formula $E=210$
\end_inset
 GPa and 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=7800$
\end_inset
 kg/m
\begin_inset Formula $^{3}$
\end_inset
, gives about 437 Hz: almost an A4.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This law tells how a set of reeds must grow from treble to bass. Going down one octave at constant thickness asks for a length multiplied by 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{2}\approx1.41$
\end_inset
; at constant length, it asks for half the thickness. Over the four or five octaves of an accordion, neither can be kept constant: the tongues would become too long, or too thin. The models of the repository use a simple rule, geometric similarity: all three dimensions scale like 
\begin_inset Formula $1/f$
\end_inset
, so the mass scales like 
\begin_inset Formula $1/f^{3}$
\end_inset
. Whether real reed makers follow a continuous law or change thickness by steps, register by register, is a hypothesis to test.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The scaling matters for more than pitch. At fixed dimensions, the amplitude that a given pressure can drive falls like 
\begin_inset Formula $1/\omega^{2}$
\end_inset
. When a tongue cut for a low A was simply retuned higher in the model, it stopped modulating anything above 185 Hz and the reed became mute; this was heard at once when the model was played at a keyboard. A reed set must therefore change its proportions along the keyboard, and the law of this change is part of the design.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): on a complete reed set from a salvaged accordion, from bass to treble, the length, width, thickness and note of each tongue (a calliper, a feeler gauge, the tuner of the repository; experiment E18). On a log-log plot, geometric similarity gives three straight lines of slope 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset
; steps would show the real rule of the makers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Variable height reeds (common used reeds)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Real tongues are profiled, and the tuner shapes them with a scraper. Scraping near the rivet makes the hinge more flexible and lowers the pitch. Scraping near the tip removes mass and raises it. Bass tongues often carry a heavier tip, which lowers the pitch without making the tongue too long. The profile is therefore the main tool of the reed maker, and a model that ignores it cannot help him.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two profiles appear in this thesis. The first is the default bass tongue of the time-domain model: three sections of 0.40, 0.045 and 0.70 mm, thin in the middle and thick at the end. It gives 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{1}=100.5$
\end_inset
 Hz, close to G2 (98 Hz), the classic design of a weighted bass reed. The thin middle section, 0.045 mm, is surprising; if it were 0.45 mm the same tongue would give 92.9 Hz, an F sharp 2. It must be checked with a calliper (experiment E7). The second is the tongue measured by Ewen in 2020 for the reedgui tool (matrix.txt): four sections over 37.4 mm, thicknesses of 0.35, 0.20 and 0.275 mm, and a 1.5 mm thick tip block over the last 9 mm. Its exact first frequency is 67.47 Hz; Elmer gives 67.72 Hz.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The profile is also a trap for computations. The finite element section below shows that the two-mode Rayleigh-Ritz model, written in 2020 and used for years, is 51 per cent too high on the matrix.txt tongue: its smooth shape functions cannot bend sharply in the thin hinge just before the heavy tip, so they make the hinge too stiff. For a uniform tongue the same two functions are exact, which is why the error went unnoticed. A profiled tongue needs a method that sees its profile: exact transfer matrices, or well-chosen finite elements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): a few scrape-and-pluck tests on one tongue: where you scrape, how much material you remove, the frequency before and after (the pince.py tool reads it from a phone recording). They would turn the craft rule into numbers and test the models on the gesture that matters most to a tuner.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Inharmonicity in reeds
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The modes of a tongue are not harmonic. For a uniform tongue, the second mode is at 6.27 times the first and the third at 17.55 times, far from the 2, 3, 4 of a vibrating string. The profile spreads them even more: on the matrix.txt tongue the ratios are 12.6 and 40.3. A twisting mode sits among them: for this tongue, with the realistic rivet clamping, Elmer predicts 67.5, 842 and 2699 Hz in bending and 1677 Hz in torsion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And yet the note of a reed is harmonic: its partials sit at exact multiples of the playing frequency. These harmonics are not the modes. They are made by the air. The tongue swings almost like a pure sine at its first mode, but the opening it leaves to the air is cut in a one-way, non-linear manner: the flow is chopped, and a chopped flow is rich in harmonics, as the pulses of a siren are. In the spectrum of a plucked tongue the harmonics of the first mode also appear, because the tongue passes through its slot; the tool pince_modes.py marks them (frequency equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $k f_{1}$
\end_inset
 within 0.3 per cent) so that they are not mistaken for modes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The higher modes are hidden in the sound but not absent. They show in the attack, before the motion has settled, and in the ring of a plucked tongue. The tool pince_modes.py finds them in a phone recording: it isolates a narrow band around each peak and fits damped sinusoids with the ESPRIT method 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "roy1989esprit"
literal "true"

\end_inset
, which gives both the frequency and the damping of each mode.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the first modes and their damping measured on the matrix.txt tongue, plucked alone and then on its chamber (gestures a and b of the protocol), to compare with the predictions above (experiment E8).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
iso-frequency reeds
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two tongues can give the same note and still be different reeds. For a uniform tongue the first frequency depends on 
\begin_inset Formula $t/L^{2}$
\end_inset
 only. A tongue 0.3 mm thick and 24 mm long and a tongue 0.6 mm thick and about 34 mm long have the same first mode. But the second one is twice as thick and 41 per cent longer. It is about 2.8 times heavier for the same width, its tip bends about half as much under the same steady pressure, and it sweeps a different volume of air per millimetre of travel. Everything that depends on these quantities can differ between the two reeds at the same pitch: the starting pressure, the loudness, the attack, the air they consume.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is a design freedom. Among all the tongues that give a note, which one starts most easily, which one has the widest range between its starting and its choking pressure, which one keeps its pitch best when the player pushes? The models of this part are not yet able to answer (Section A physical model for reed design lists what they still get wrong). The question can be answered by experiment first.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): please confirm that iso-frequency reeds means reeds of the same pitch but of different geometry. If so, a few pairs of such tongues, measured on the same chamber (starting pressure, pitch at several pressures, sound level), would be the cleanest test of the models and a direct guide for design.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Material of reeds
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A reed is a spring, and its material decides two things: how stiff it is for its weight, and how long it keeps ringing once plucked. Accordion tongues are made of spring steel. The models take 
\begin_inset Formula $E=210$
\end_inset
 GPa for its Young modulus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=7800$
\end_inset
 kg/m
\begin_inset Formula $^{3}$
\end_inset
 for its density and 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu=0.29$
\end_inset
 for its Poisson ratio, usual handbook values.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Handbook values are not good enough for tuning. The frequency of a tongue depends on 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{E/\rho}$
\end_inset
, and 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset
 varies by about 3 per cent with the grade of steel. An error of 3 per cent on 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset
 is an error of 1.5 per cent on the frequency, about 26 cents: more than a quarter of a semitone. The material must therefore be measured, not looked up. The tool material.py does it with a uniform strip of the same steel: clamp it, excite it, read its first frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{1}$
\end_inset
, and solve the cantilever formula for 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E=12\,\rho\left(\frac{2\pi f_{1}L^{2}}{(\beta_{1}L)^{2}\,t}\right)^{2}
\]

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The thickness must be measured with care: since 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{1}$
\end_inset
 is proportional to 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
, an error of 1 per cent on the thickness becomes an error of 2 per cent on 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second property is damping: how fast the ringing dies. It is written as a damping ratio 
\begin_inset Formula $\zeta$
\end_inset
, or as a quality factor 
\begin_inset Formula $Q=1/(2\zeta)$
\end_inset
. The models use 
\begin_inset Formula $\zeta=0.004$
\end_inset
 (
\begin_inset Formula $Q\approx125$
\end_inset
), a guessed value. It can be measured with a phone. The tool pince.py finds each pluck in a recording, keeps a narrow band around the first mode, fits the decay of its envelope, and returns the frequency, 
\begin_inset Formula $\zeta$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset
; pince_modes.py does the same for the next modes. One trap is worth knowing: the automatic gain of a phone raises a sound that is dying, and it can make a tongue look less damped than it is. The frequency is not affected. Two plucks of different strength give a check.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The damping measured on a plucked reed is not only the damping of the steel. It also includes the losses in the rivet and the clamping, and the air around the tongue. Separating these parts is a question for later; for the models, the total is what counts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the Young modulus of the steel of your reed bank (one uniform strip, material.py), and the damping of the first modes of a few tongues (plucked alone, gesture a of the protocol). These two numbers replace the handbook value and the guessed 
\begin_inset Formula $\zeta$
\end_inset
 in every model of this part.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The reed socket
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The tongue does not live alone. It is riveted to a plate, over a slot, and the plate is waxed onto the reed block. We call socket everything that holds the tongue and shapes the air around it: the rivet, the slot, the plate, the gap around the tongue, the lift of the tip, and the leather valve. Small details of the socket decide whether a reed speaks well.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rivet first. A rivet is not a perfect wall. In the finite element model, a realistic clamping, where the heel of the tongue rests on the plate and is held only under the head of the rivet, lowers the first frequency by 0.3 per cent compared with a perfect clamping at the root of the slot (Section Finite element model with fluid structure interaction).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then the gap. Around the tongue, inside the slot, the air passes through a gap of a few hundredths of a millimetre (30 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset
m in the models, a guessed value). In such a narrow passage the air is sticky. The Reynolds number, which compares inertia with viscosity, is about 
\begin_inset Formula $vh/\nu\approx40\times3\cdot10^{-5}/1.5\cdot10^{-5}\approx80$
\end_inset
 for an air speed 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset
 of 40 m/s in a gap 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset
 of 30 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset
m. At this value viscosity rules, and the law of Bernoulli, which describes a jet with no friction, is the wrong law: the flow follows the law of Poiseuille for a thin slit. A two-dimensional flow computation with Elmer confirms it: in the 30 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset
m gap, 80 per cent of the pressure drop is viscous. The same computation gives the steady flow through the slot at 1000 Pa within 0.4 per cent of the one-dimensional model that combines Poiseuille in the gap and Bernoulli outside it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Out of the gap, where the tongue has left the slot, Bernoulli becomes valid again. The flow follows the motion of the tongue without delay as long as the Strouhal number 
\begin_inset Formula $fh/v$
\end_inset
 stays small: about 
\begin_inset Formula $440\times0.5\cdot10^{-3}/40\approx0.006$
\end_inset
 for an A4. The jet narrows after the sharp edge of the slot; the models take a contraction coefficient of 0.61, a value borrowed from steady jets and not checked for an opening that oscillates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The lift and the thickness of the plate are guessed in the models: 1.5 and 3 times the thickness of the tongue. The leather valve, which stops the reverse flow, is taken as perfect: in reality it has a mass, it leaks a little and it slaps.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally, an experiment by Ewen shows that the socket can change everything. A tongue cut in a steel sheet and held between two plates, a sandwich, plays in both directions of the air. And its pitch depends on the height of the supports on each side: the same tongue gives an A when blown and a B when sucked, a whole tone apart, 12 per cent in frequency. Two harmonium plates mounted head to head, which normally play in one direction only, also play both ways. The next section shows that this simple experiment refutes the models that explain the normal reed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the gap around the tongue, the lift and the thickness of the plate of a few reeds (feeler gauges, grazing light, calliper; experiment E6 of the two-reed audit); and for the sandwich: the dimensions of the tongue, the height of each support, the gap, the pitch when plucked, the pitch in each direction at a few pressures read on a water manometer, and how the air was given (mouth, box or bellows). A slow-motion phone video (240 frames per second) of a bass reed would show whether the tip leaves the faces of the plates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
A physical model for reed design
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A physical model is a small world in which one thing can be changed at a time. Its purpose here is practical: to design a reed, its profile and its chamber, on paper, and to know beforehand how it will speak. This section tells how the model of the repository was built, and how it was proven wrong several times. The story matters as much as the result: each time, nature answered what it is, not what was expected.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The model has five ingredients. The tongue, described by its modes. The flow through the opening, by Bernoulli, in one direction only. The opening law, which says how wide the passage is for a given position of the tongue. The chamber, an air spring whose pressure rises when more air comes in than goes out. And the source, the bellows or the turbine of the bench, with its internal impedance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The starting point was Ewen's MATLAB code, ported to Python (banc_recherche/reed_model.py). A careful audit showed that it could not oscillate, for reasons that each teach something. The law of the chamber was inverted: air was injected and the pressure went down, so the tongue was sucked against the plate 99.2 per cent of the time. Written properly, with the pressure as the variable of the chamber, the equation becomes stiff: the air spring of a small chamber is so hard (
\begin_inset Formula $\rho c^{2}/V_{0}\approx1.8\cdot10^{10}$
\end_inset
 Pa/m
\begin_inset Formula $^{3}$
\end_inset
) that a standard Runge-Kutta scheme at 44.1 kHz explodes. It needs eight times smaller steps. The opening had no lower and no upper bound, so a tongue could pass 10 cm through its plate without the code noticing. And the damping matrix was not symmetric, so in some directions it injected energy instead of removing it. The corrected model (banc_recherche/reed_oscillator.py) fixes these four points: pressure as state variable, eight times oversampling, an opening bounded on both sides and open in one direction only, and a symmetric damping built from the true modes of the tongue. Nine tests check it, among them the conservation of mass and the absence of any numerical blow-up.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One tool changed the method. Instead of simulating a second of sound to see whether a reed starts, the model is linearised around its rest position, and the eigenvalues of the linear system are read. If the largest growth rate is negative, the rest is stable and the reed stays silent; if it is positive, the reed starts, and the imaginary part gives its starting frequency. A search on the flow then finds the starting threshold. One evaluation takes about one millisecond instead of about one second of simulation. Each new hypothesis can be tested in a minute.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first twist was a piston that should not be there. The balance of the chamber contained a term for the volume swept by the tongue, as if the tongue were a piston in the wall of the chamber. This is right for a beating reed, like a clarinet reed pressed on its mouthpiece. It is wrong for a free reed: the tongue sits in its own slot, and the air it moves on one side is taken back at once through the slot itself. It does not compress its chamber; it modulates an opening. With this term, the model played a 102.1 Hz tongue at 119.2 Hz, 267 cents too high, a minor third, at every pressure. Without it, the note comes back to the blade, and the small remaining shift fades towards the treble, as it should (Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:hauteur-languette"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
). The real chamber, 7.9 cm
\begin_inset Formula $^{3}$
\end_inset
 (35 by 15 by 15 mm), is now enough to start; before, the model needed five times this volume. And the note now goes down when the player pushes, as on every accordion: 
\begin_inset Formula $-15.7$
\end_inset
 cents between 1.3 and 2 times the starting flow.
\end_layout

