\begin_layout Standard
This twist came with a lesson about tests. Three of the fifteen tests of the model protected the error they should have caught. One accepted a playing frequency up to 1.4 times the frequency of the blade, that is up to 580 cents: wide enough to let a minor third pass. A test that freezes a behaviour without confronting it with a measurable quantity freezes a bug as well as a law.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What is still wrong in the single-reed model is said plainly. The starting pressure covers three decades over the keyboard, from 1.80 Pa at 102 Hz to 1781 Pa at 880 Hz, where a real accordion seems to need roughly one decade, of the order of 100 to 1000 Pa everywhere (an estimate from playing, to be measured). And the amplitude is no longer bounded: the travel of the tip, from one extreme to the other, reaches 2.15 mm at twice the starting flow and 47.4 mm at sixteen times, for a tongue 55 mm long, which is absurd. The term that was removed also acted as a brake; without it, there is no other limiting mechanism in the model. The most likely missing piece explains both faults at once: the model applies the pressure on the whole tongue whatever its position, whereas a tongue that has left its slot sits in the jet and no longer feels the full pressure drop.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second step put two reeds together, as in a real note with two voices: two tongues, two chambers, the holes in the soundboard, the common channel under the pallet, and the pallet (banc_recherche/coupled_reeds.py). Each element is a quantity that a calliper can measure: a volume, or a mass of air 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho\ell/S$
\end_inset
 for a duct of length 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell$
\end_inset
 and section 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset
, with its losses. With these honest losses, no reed starts at all. This is consistent with Ricot, Caussé and Misdariis 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "ricot2005"
literal "true"

\end_inset
: the starting of an accordion reed comes from the flow in its slot, and a model with a uniform pressure on the tongue does not contain it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A geometric idea then made the model start without any tuned resistance. Suppose that, at rest, the tip is lifted on the side the air comes from, so that the air first pushes the tongue into its slot: the opening closes as the tongue advances, then opens again on the other side. When the tongue advances, the flow slows down; the mass of air along the path resists this slowing, and turns it into an overpressure in phase with the velocity of the tongue, the push on the swing. The prediction was made before the check, and the check removed one ingredient at a time (Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:section-qui-se-ferme"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
). Only the two predicted ingredients matter, the closing opening and the mass of air, and there is an optimum of mass: too much air mass decouples the tongue from the flow.
\end_layout

