\begin_layout Standard
With this mechanism the model predicts, for guessed dimensions: a start between 200 and 1000 Pa from A2 to A5, a playing pitch a few cents below the blade (
\begin_inset Formula $-4$
\end_inset
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $-24$
\end_inset
 cents), and a pitch that goes down when the pressure rises. It also gives results that are clearly wrong: a travel of 4 to 5 mm for an A4 tongue, 0.4 L/s of air per reed, and a sound efficiency of 1 to 3 per cent. Something brakes the real tongue that the model does not have. The audit of the two-reed model classifies every element as derived, checked, hypothesis, guessed or borrowed, and lists the cheap experiments that can contradict it (research/docs/audit_deux_anches.md). The publications that inspired it, Ricot et al. 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "ricot2005"
literal "true"

\end_inset
 and Millot and Baumann 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "millot2007"
literal "true"

\end_inset
, were taken as starting ideas, not as proofs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The third step looked at the slot on both sides of the tongue (banc_recherche/slot_stack.py). The slot crosses a stack of supports; the tongue can sit anywhere in it; the air crosses three parts in series: the column of air before the tongue, the passage around it (the viscous gap while it is inside the stack, the curtain between its edge and the edge of the slot when it is out), and the column after it. This single description covers the normal reed, the same reed with the air reversed, and the sandwich. The result is clear. The normal mounting plays, with a pitch 
\begin_inset Formula $-9$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $-14$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $-26$
\end_inset
 cents below the blade at 300, 1000 and 3000 Pa. The reversed mounting does not play, as expected. But the sandwich never plays, whatever the heights of the supports and the pressure, even when the pressure pushes the tip 0.25 mm out of the downstream face.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So the model is refuted by the sandwich. In a flow that is one-dimensional and instantaneous, a tongue centred in its slot has no source of energy. Since the real sandwich plays, the real mechanism involves what this model does not contain: a delay of the flow at the edge of the tongue (the jet forming and separating, small vortices) and a pressure that is not uniform along the tongue. The closing-opening mechanism is at best a special case: it reproduces the accordion mounting, it does not explain the sandwich. A test of the repository records this disagreement instead of hiding it; it will pass on the day the model makes a sandwich play.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The next experiment is numerical: an unsteady two-dimensional flow around a moving tongue, with the coupling between the air and the tongue (research/cfd/sandwich2d, Elmer and Gmsh, free software). The steady case is validated. The oscillating case moves the tongue by 
\begin_inset Formula $\pm5$
\end_inset
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset
m at 440 Hz and computes the work of the air on the tongue at each cycle, and the damping and stiffness that the air adds, to be compared with those of the steel. If the air gives energy to a flat, centred tongue, the computation will show where it comes from.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where does this leave the reed designer? The modal part of the model can be trusted, with the right method for profiled tongues. The trend of the pitch with pressure is the same in every version: the note goes down when the player pushes. The stability analysis, which says whether a reed starts and at what frequency, is fast and reliable for a given model. But the starting pressures, the amplitude, the loudness and the air consumption are not yet predicted. They wait for the measurements of the reed itself, and for the missing mechanism.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the measurements that can contradict the models first, in this order: the pitch of a tongue plucked and then played at several pressures (E1 of the two-reed audit), the beat of two reeds of one note tuned one at a time and then together (E3), and the sandwich measurements of Section The reed socket. Then the results of the oscillating flow computation, when it has run.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Finite element model with fluid structure interaction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A free reed is a steel tongue riveted over a slot, a few tens of millimetres long and a few tenths of a millimetre thick, often scraped thin near the rivet and loaded with a heavier tip. Its first bending mode sets the pitch; the next ones and the torsion decide how the tongue answers the jet. This section describes how these modes were computed with the finite element method, how the computation was checked against exact solutions, and why one earlier result, kept for years as a reference, turned out to be wrong. The finite element model is passive: it gives the modes of the tongue in vacuo and the acoustic response of the chamber seen from the slot. The jet through the slot, non-linear and one-way, stays in the semi-analytical model, which receives these results.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All computations use Elmer 26.2 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "elmer_models"
literal "true"

\end_inset
, meshed with Gmsh 4.15 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "geuzaine2009gmsh"
literal "true"

\end_inset
; the scripts are in research/fem of the repository, and every number below is regenerated by them.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Geometry, material and boundary conditions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A tongue is described in the reed bank as a series of sections from the rivet to the tip, each with its length, its width and its thickness at both ends, and its material; a tip mass can be added. The reference tongue of this chapter is the one Ewen measured in 2020 for the reedgui tool (matrix.txt): four sections over a free length of 37.4 mm, widths from 4.26 to 3.47 mm, thicknesses of 0.35, 0.20 and 0.275 mm, and a 1.5 mm thick tip block over the last 9 mm. Spring steel is taken with 
\begin_inset Formula $E=210$
\end_inset
 GPa, 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=7800$
\end_inset
 kg/m
\begin_inset Formula $^{3}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu=0.29$
\end_inset
; handbook values vary by about 3 per cent on 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset
 with the grade.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two clampings are compared. The ideal one fixes the whole cross-section at the root of the slot. The realistic one, called rivet clamping, extends the heel 5 mm behind the slot, lets it rest on the plate (no vertical displacement under the heel) and holds it at every point over a 2 mm band under the head of the rivet, 3 mm behind the slot. The realistic clamping lowers 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{1}$
\end_inset
 by 0.3 per cent: small, but larger than the remaining discretisation error, so it is the default. The eigenproblem is linear elasticity, solved with the direct solver Umfpack (MUMPS is not shipped with Elmer 26.2).
\end_layout

