\begin_inset Formula $f_{1}$
\end_inset
 (0.70 to 0.87 per cent on modes 1 to 5), and on a square section of 
\begin_inset Formula $0.3\times0.3$
\end_inset
 mm, where there is no plate effect, by 0.13 per cent only. Shells or plates would bring nothing here: the quadratic solid is already fast and it also gives torsion and in-plane bending.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Why the 2019 reference of 144.4 Hz was wrong: locking
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In 2019 a Code_Aster study of a heavy-tip bass tongue, drawn in CAD, gave a first frequency of 144.4 Hz, and this value was kept as the finite element reference. The same mesh was reused in 2020 in an Elmer study (bass_reed): linear four-node tetrahedra, 19 408 nodes, and a single element through the 0.10 mm thickness of the thinnest part. Reading the geometry back from the mesh gives a free length of 37.5 mm, a width tapering from 4.33 to 3.35 mm, a thickness going from 0.335 down to 0.10 mm and back up to 0.248 mm, and a 1.4 mm thick steel tip over the last 9 mm. On this geometry the exact Euler-Bernoulli solution gives 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{1}=25.09$
\end_inset
 Hz and Elmer with quadratic hexahedra 25.23 Hz. Code_Aster was 5.7 times too high on 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{1}$
\end_inset
, that is 33 times too stiff (Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:maillage"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
b).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The solver was not at fault. The old Elmer case, run again on Elmer 26.2 with the same mesh (Umfpack replacing MUMPS), gives 140.1 Hz with its own material constants (200 GPa, 7850 kg/m
\begin_inset Formula $^{3}$
\end_inset
), that is 144.0 Hz once brought to the constants of the Code_Aster run: same mesh, same error, two independent codes. The cause is the element. A four-node tetrahedron has a constant strain field. In bending, the axial strain varies linearly through the thickness and vanishes on the neutral plane; with one constant-strain element through the thickness, the mesh cannot represent this gradient and can only bend by storing spurious shear energy. The discrete structure is far too stiff: this is shear locking 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "zienkiewicz2013"
literal "true"

\end_inset
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "macneal1994"
literal "true"

\end_inset
. It grows with the aspect ratio of the elements, here about ten (one millimetre in plane for a tenth of a millimetre in thickness), and it strikes hardest where the tongue is thinnest, the hinge that sets the first mode: 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{1}$
\end_inset
 is wrong by a factor 5.7, 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{2}$
\end_inset
 by 2.2. Quadratic elements represent a linear strain variation exactly, which is why the 20-node hexahedra, with three layers through the thickness, agree with the beam solution to a few tenths of a per cent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The lesson is general. A computation can be internally consistent, reproducible on two codes, and still wrong. The only protection is an independent reference computed before trusting the mesh; here the exact beam solution played that role.
\end_layout

