\begin_layout Subsection
The chamber seen from the slot
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The air of a chamber of the R12 reed block (two cells and the table hole above them) is meshed with second-order tetrahedra; a scalar Helmholtz problem does not lock. The walls and plates are rigid, the far end of the table hole is a pressure-release surface placed one end correction (0.85 times the equivalent radius, flanged) beyond the hole, and the slot of the tested reed is the input port. The input impedance 
\begin_inset Formula $Z(f)=p/q$
\end_inset
 is what the chamber, the hole and the outside do to the tongue, frequency by frequency.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Sweeping 70 frequencies took 487 s for one chamber. A faster route gives the same answer: the poles of 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset
 come from one eigenvalue computation, the residues from 14 harmonic solutions taken away from the poles, and 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset
 is rebuilt in Foster form, a series inertance plus a sum of lossless resonators:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Z(\omega)=\mathrm{i}\omega\left[L_{f}+\sum_{n}\frac{a_{n}}{\omega_{n}^{2}-\omega^{2}}\right]
\]

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On the same mesh it reproduces the sweep within 0.017 per cent (median) in 53 s instead of 487 s. The mesh of 2 mm is within 0.06 per cent of the finest one computed on 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{H}$
\end_inset
, and the effective volume moves by less than 0.1 per cent. The Helmholtz solver itself was checked on a rigid box (
\begin_inset Formula $c/2L=3430.0$
\end_inset
 Hz exactly) and on a 100 mm duct, whose impedance 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{i}\rho c\tan(kL)/S$
\end_inset
 is recovered to 
\begin_inset Formula $1.3\times10^{-5}$
\end_inset
. The first resonator gives the parameters of the lumped network: effective volume 
\begin_inset Formula $V=\rho c^{2}/a_{1}$
\end_inset
 and effective hole length 
\begin_inset Formula $l=(a_{1}/\omega_{1}^{2})S/\rho$
\end_inset
. For chamber 1, under the bass tongue: 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{H}=1139.7$
\end_inset
 Hz, 
\begin_inset Formula $V=17.79$
\end_inset
 cm
\begin_inset Formula $^{3}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $l=25.8$
\end_inset
 mm.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The main physical result is about the sign. At the frequency of the tongue, far below 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{H}$
\end_inset
, the open chamber behaves as a mass of air (phase +90 degrees): the air pushed by the tongue escapes through the table hole, and what the tongue feels is the inertia of that air. A closed cavity, as in the time-domain model inherited from the MATLAB code (7.875 cm
\begin_inset Formula $^{3}$
\end_inset
), behaves as a spring (phase 
\begin_inset Formula $-$
\end_inset
90 degrees). In the band of the tongue, the impedance of the closed cavity is wrong by 12 400 per cent for the bass reed (median), 494 per cent for the medium and 232 per cent for the high one; one Foster resonator brings the error below 9 per cent, two to 0.5 per cent or less.
\end_layout

